    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_0d0c77e75d3e3fb5d7126bac914efc6a46bedd14d2744317c28f7e2184e8367e",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Coefficient support is a spectral-resolution budget: for degree r, conductor N, and height T, the source's density estimate and uncertainty argument require coefficients through roughly N(T/2π)^r, while in the self-dual setting the restriction to even tests and corresponding division in the formula reduce that range to its square root. This makes typical cost comparable to an approximate functional equation, but local crowding rather than height alone can dominate; only heuristic random-matrix reasoning supports the warning that rare unusually small normalized gaps may require many more coefficients. Compact support removes error terms from this explicit-formula evaluation, yet it does not remove the separate RH assumption needed by the minimization. The method is therefore conditionally rigorous under RH and especially useful as a fast low-zero locator and diagnostic before the subsequent rigorous certification layer, not as an unconditional absolute zero count.",
