    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_2e83b7a552ab656a46cf66ae3f8819b4d88df2e75060debbb4b25e8ca1cd47fc",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Brauer's quotient makes numerical evaluation possible but exposes only zeros minus poles. The Heilbronn character transports every possible local zero/pole decomposition into the virtual-character lattice, where holomorphy of monomial L-functions imposes nonnegative pairings; almost monomiality then makes an observed irreducible character indecomposable in that feasible cone. Under the tested simple, distinct-zero pattern, this turns a lossy net count into the actual local count, and finite lattice inequalities make the group condition checkable.",
