    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_e50dfec6cf25cab9f77536b02c93f7263654af74c13d3551a803ca25d5e739a9",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The computation deliberately separates inexpensive spectral guidance from certification. A positive explicit-formula quadratic form uses finite coefficient data to localize or exclude candidate ordinates; a rigorously error-bounded evaluator then produces interval-certified critical-line sign changes; finally Turing's integrated bounds on the mean argument recover the absolute zero count, so agreement with the sign-change count certifies completeness. Explicit-formula agreement remains diagnostic, while proof strength enters through interval control and the Turing count.",
